  "text": "At x=1,\n- 1^2 = (q(1))^2 + 32 (Multiply by 2)\n- (q(1))^2 = -33\n=> (q(1))^2 = 33\n=> q(1) = (33^0.5) or (-33^0.5)\n\nAt x=2,\n- 1^4 = (q(2))^2 + 32 (Multiply by 12)\n- (q(2))^2 = -27\n=> (q(2))^2 = 27\n=> q(2) = (27^0.5) or (-27^0.5) But x=1 can only have one value since it is a real-valued function.\n\na Domain: {2,1,-2,-1}\n\nลอง\nLet a,b be the values of x, x^4 equal to a and a-4, when converted to a and x^2, it all comes to the same function (q(x))^2 + 32, which means a-b is equal\n\nTherefore\nq(x) = (sqrt1)^10 or (-sqrt1)^10 (1) and q(x) = (sqrt2)^10 or (-sqrt2)^10 (2)\nq(x) = (sqrt3)^10 or (-sqrt3)^10 (3)\n... and so on.\nThis gives us 7 possible solutions.\n\nIt can be verified that q(x) = sign(x)*c, where c = 3^1/2 or -3^1/2 is a correct domain, though not x = 0 case.\n1. The polynomial q(x) should have a domain that is closed under the properties of (x^4) and (x^4-4), which means the roots include all values of x^2 and x^2-4. Hence the roots are rational (2) and irrational (-sqrt3, -sqrt5, sqrt5, sqrt3).\n\n2. The roots must include ±5, as (a^4-b^4) can simplify to (a-b)(a+b)(a^3+b^3). find the or the",
